course: dictionary
lesson: 10357
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. Trap is a noun (a thing) and a verb (an action), and it has several senses. Read the words around it to find out which sense the writer means. «Animals can get trapped in plastic rings by the neck, beak or legs» tells you that trapped means stuck, with no way out.
explain: Trap can also mean a device for catching animals, or a clever plan to catch or trick someone. In a story, a trap builds tension, so the reader wonders whether someone will be caught or whether a clever hero will escape. When you meet trap, ask yourself: is this a thing, a plan, or an action of catching?
explain: Some phrases always go together, such as set a trap, fall into a trap and be trapped. A trap question works in a different way: it hides a detail that tricks you into a wrong answer. Try each meaning in the sentence. The one that still makes sense is the right one.
fact: Trap can be a noun (a trap, traps) or a verb (trap, traps, trapped, trapping).
fact: As a noun, «trap» can mean a device for catching animals, such as a mousetrap.
fact: «Trap» can also mean a clever plan to catch or trick someone.
fact: «Trapped» means caught, or kept somewhere it cannot get out of.
fact: A thinking trap is a habit of thought that bends the truth, making things seem worse, bigger or more certain than they are.
exercise: «Carbon dioxide traps heat like a blanket.» What does traps mean here? | Lets heat out quickly | Turns heat into cold air | *Keeps heat in, like a blanket | Makes a loud noise
exercise: «Animals can get trapped in plastic rings by the neck, beak or legs.» What does trapped mean? | Carried gently | Painted bright | *Caught, with no way out | Happy and free
exercise: «A thinking trap is a habit of thought that bends the truth.» What is a thinking trap? | A device for catching thoughts | A clever plan to win a game | A puzzle with a hidden answer | *A habit of thought that bends the truth
test: «People call pest experts or set traps away from children and pets.» What does set traps mean? | *Place devices to catch pests, away from children and pets | Lock the door tight | Hide the food from the cat | Dig a hole in the garden
test: What is a trap question? | A question with a very long answer | A question asked by a police officer | *A question with a hidden detail that changes the answer | A question that nobody can answer
exam: «The pirates set a trap, and the captain fell into it.» In this sentence, trap means | A device for catching fish | A part of a question | *A clever plan to catch or trick someone | A narrow path through the hills
